#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6238f33-a5cb-54a5-8078-db53f6c26b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351633} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ef841b-785b-5756-b6e5-3fee2681a0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351634} {:answer/id #uuid "74f4e605-7fc2-508c-a8ef-e65f46034979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351635} {:answer/id #uuid "d6481527-626a-5507-887b-50b89f8b1191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351636} {:answer/id #uuid "954ef567-396b-5cbf-95a8-65d48dc360c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6481527-626a-5507-887b-50b89f8b1191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351636} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90d392a-ce4d-5aa7-9f9e-f4ef9b94d16f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f6d9f66-8b90-5e43-ac23-53d484fde86e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161100" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/6ddc0b2138025b07ffdb7bf34afabeba578400b194922bec7a733cc9246c7c0f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=2-x,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=2-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-x \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/587c299c1bdf89061be205cf185d6335fba865d2e30c81cac5b9d223fc84b486.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e04ce5-6aef-5fda-b242-ffe936947102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351640} {:answer/id #uuid "a3df5412-2265-5af0-aa2c-700082253549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351641} {:answer/id #uuid "7501973f-ef79-585a-8c13-b2e77ffa5cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351642} {:answer/id #uuid "79e45fd2-a572-5168-af78-bdcf54d3b629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351643} {:answer/id #uuid "327f0207-ddcd-50da-a59b-54817a6c6332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7501973f-ef79-585a-8c13-b2e77ffa5cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351642} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db71b5a-fb3a-5cf6-9c7e-1f8da3303b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34d05c35-86b0-569b-abb1-83dd28e01a64" :question/math-academy-id "q-161115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/636c9e587d6cbb9fcf9b24d068520330d5365cf80a2800bdd8244698512be3c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{3/2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{r}]}_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89ff808b-4714-5ad3-bb66-fb3bcf44c5a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351647} {:answer/id #uuid "24c42e3b-6e5c-55f4-a8bd-77da7d552f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351648} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc6a9f1-d7be-5ef8-a015-bcd74f1f19e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351649} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f61055-2925-5ce8-b476-1fbfd7890804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351650} {:answer/id #uuid "b0216222-7a8c-5c99-bdc0-b8f425c8fe46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ff808b-4714-5ad3-bb66-fb3bcf44c5a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351647} :answer-field/id #uuid "085faeb2-ecea-56f4-b032-2789c1f67e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43eb012c-c603-5435-8700-fbf81cd78381" :question/math-academy-id "q-83781" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/959705e66be54eff155f5e784f5a42c8fb5098b9fdf9ec877b794f196bd674bf.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the fourth quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa01155-0abe-5bbe-aca2-c05230ed9593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351654} {:answer/id #uuid "12ae6d96-175e-53ba-a6b1-1f3f0a415ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351655} {:answer/id #uuid "71f0b8ff-dd03-5773-8399-b422dae23139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351656} {:answer/id #uuid "03c74dad-e3d6-51b6-9da3-1e7aca95a404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351657} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb59947-3596-55ec-9a6e-e7a225e1043d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa01155-0abe-5bbe-aca2-c05230ed9593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351654} :answer-field/id #uuid "47541f18-82a3-515f-9619-9ef8678cd853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac4dd0c8-f7de-5672-a734-787cfdb60520" :question/math-academy-id "q-83799" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/ce35f6ef14576f536c268dd7420af6e0226609ab5305e85770f400439c2435de.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$  So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/f518a206638071a7b8548882a453123b6d78aed0aa94cc0e6e23429f22c6f1b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e23ee05d-042a-5350-bea0-ab5e285a70be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351661} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51ec98-94b6-58e7-a83a-d0321e2191ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351662} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fa6b89-feb5-56df-baf5-efdd691f3cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351663} {:answer/id #uuid "e16519b7-6b06-5e75-8704-8f648764b813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351664} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8d6f9-5254-5050-8692-330c9ee962a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8d6f9-5254-5050-8692-330c9ee962a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65b0b61-bd97-5ed3-b644-cbe27a096364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ca7e6b-3cdb-56e9-925d-725cb9a41ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065827/assets/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
